 "name": "Urban Regeneration Streetscapes: Perception and Emotion",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: twelve street-level photos of the regenerated Yulin neighbourhood in Chengdu, three each dominated by vegetation (A1-A3), buildings (B1-B3), roads (C1-C3) or facilities such as benches, kiosks and signs (D1-D3). Participants first answer the paper's background questions (gender, age, education, occupation, origin, landscape-architecture major, visits to Yulin), then view all 12 photos in random order for 20 s each with a 5 s blank screen between them. They then rate every photo on the paper's 10 semantic-differential pairs (e.g. sparse - abundant vegetation, dangerous - safe, ugly - beautiful, dim - vibrant) on a 7-point scale from -3 to +3 (completely / very / quite / neutral), and finally rate the emotion each photo evokes on 9-point valence (very unpleasant - very pleasant) and arousal (very not excited - very excited) scales with the Self-Assessment Manikin figures. Scene order in the two questionnaires follows the paper's supplement. Rebuilt from Cheng, Wang, Xu, Ge & Ma (2026), Assessment of perceived environmental quality in urban regeneration: integrating neurophysiological and psychological perspectives, Frontiers in Psychology 17: 1874589; the photos, manikin pictures and all question wording come from its Supplementary Material (CC BY 4.0). Image ids follow the paper's Figure 2 (A1-D3). Analysis as in the paper: one-way ANOVA of each SD item, the SD total, valence and arousal across the four element groups with LSD post-hoc tests; the paper (27 participants) found significant group differences for all except Safety, with vegetation scenes most pleasant and facility scenes most distinctive, beautiful, vibrant and arousing. Differences from the original study: (1) online, without the eye tracker and EEG headset, so fixation and EEG measures are not recorded and there is no 6-minute recovery phase; (2) the supplement's typo 'Commerciallviable' is shown as 'Commercially viable'; (3) the manikin rows are mirrored so that they run from 1 on the left to 9 on the right like the number scale; (4) the photos are the supplement's copies (about 550 px wide), not the full-resolution camera images; (5) the name, gender and age fields repeated at the top of the SD sheet are not asked again; (6) the welcome text and the viewing instruction are our wording.",
